@page "/components/scrollablepane"

<PageOutlet Url="components/scrollablepane"
            Title="ScrollablePane"
            Description="ScrollablePane is a box that scrolls whatever is put in it: sized, axis-aware, themed scrollbars, edge fades, snapping, drag and wheel scrolling, scroll events, a scrolling API, and a reader's place kept as content arrives." />

<div>
    <DemoPage Name="ScrollablePane"
              SecondaryNames="@(["ScrollView", "ScrollArea", "ScrollViewer"])"
              Description="ScrollablePane is a box that scrolls whatever is put in it, with the browser's own scrolling - momentum, keyboard and assistive technology included. Around that it sizes the box, picks the axes, stops scroll chaining, draws the scrollbar in the theme's colors and hides it until it is wanted, fades the edges that have more beyond them, snaps onto items, scrolls a strip with a drag or a wheel, and becomes a keyboard stop of its own. It reports its position, the start and end of each scroll and each edge it reaches; it scrolls from code; and it stays pinned to a growing log or holds still while older content loads above."
              Parameters="componentParameters"
              PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
              CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
              SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
              SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
              GitHubUrl="Surfaces/ScrollablePane/BitScrollablePane.razor"
              GitHubDemoUrl="Surfaces/ScrollablePane/BitScrollablePaneDemo.razor">
        <NotesTemplate>
            <BitText>
                A pane that only sizes a box, picks its axes, reserves a gutter, snaps or styles its scrollbar is pure
                CSS: one element, no JavaScript. The browser side is set up only for <b>AutoScroll</b>, <b>Fade</b>,
                <b>DragScroll</b>, <b>HorizontalWheel</b>, <b>PreserveScroll</b>, <b>AutoHideScrollbar</b> and the
                scroll and edge callbacks.
            </BitText>
            <br />
            <BitText>
                <b>Smooth</b> collapses to an instant move under <i>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</i> - use the
                ForceAnimation toggle at the top of this page to see it animate anyway.
            </BitText>
        </NotesTemplate>

        <Examples>
            <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
                <div>
                    Give the pane a size its content does not fit in; the rest scrolls with the platform's own scrollbar,
                    wheel, touch and keyboard handling. Content that fits shows no scrollbar.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="10rem" Class="pane">
                    <p>
                        Every story starts with a blank canvas, a quiet space waiting to be filled with ideas, emotions, and dreams.
                        These placeholder words symbolize the beginning - a moment of possibility where creativity has yet to take shape.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In the beginning, there is silence, a blank canvas yearning to be filled, a quiet space where creativity waits
                        to awaken. Think of this text as a bridge, connecting the empty spaces of now with the narratives of tomorrow.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In this space, potential reigns supreme. It is a moment suspended in time, where imagination dances freely and
                        each word has the power to transform into something extraordinary.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For now, these lines are here to remind you of the beauty of beginnings. They are the quiet before the symphony,
                        the foundation upon which your creativity will build.
                    </p>
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Dimensions" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
                <div>
                    <b>Width</b>, <b>Height</b>, <b>MinWidth</b>, <b>MinHeight</b>, <b>MaxWidth</b> and <b>MaxHeight</b>
                    take any CSS length. Leave the height unset and cap it with <b>MaxHeight</b> for a pane that grows with
                    its content and scrolls once it is full. <b>FullWidth</b> / <b>FullHeight</b> / <b>FullSize</b> (100%),
                    <b>FitWidth</b> / <b>FitHeight</b> / <b>FitSize</b> (fit-content) and <b>AutoWidth</b> /
                    <b>AutoHeight</b> / <b>AutoSize</b> (auto) cover the common cases.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <div>Width and Height:</div><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Width="18rem" Height="6rem" Class="pane">
                    <p>
                        Once upon a time, stories wove connections between people, a symphony of voices crafting shared dreams.
                        Each word carried meaning, each pause brought understanding, and every story found its listener.
                    </p>
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br /><br />
                <div>MaxHeight, with a growing number of lines:</div><br />
                <BitButton OnClick="() => maxHeightLines++">Add a line</BitButton>
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => maxHeightLines = 2">Reset</BitButton>
                <br /><br />
                <BitScrollablePane MaxHeight="10rem" Class="pane">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= maxHeightLines; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Line @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Overflow & Horizontal" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
                <div>
                    <b>Overflow</b> sets both axes: <b>Auto</b> scrolls only when needed, <b>Scroll</b> always shows the
                    scrollbar, <b>Hidden</b> clips, <b>Visible</b> spills out of the box. <b>OverflowX</b> and
                    <b>OverflowY</b> have the last word on their axis. <b>NoScroll</b> clips and ignores the wheel, drags
                    and keys, while the content stays interactive and the scrolling API still works.
                </div>
                <br />
                <div>
                    <b>Horizontal</b> keeps the content on one line (<i>white-space: nowrap</i>) and scrolls it sideways
                    only. It leaves the layout alone, so lay block children out with a flex row of your own.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitChoiceGroup @bind-Value="overflow"
                                Horizontal
                                Label="Overflow"
                                TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitOverflow>" TValue="BitOverflow">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Auto" Value="BitOverflow.Auto" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hidden" Value="BitOverflow.Hidden" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Scroll" Value="BitOverflow.Scroll" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Visible" Value="BitOverflow.Visible" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="noScroll" Label="NoScroll" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Overflow="overflow" NoScroll="noScroll" Height="12rem" Width="20rem" Class="pane">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Item @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br /><br />
                <div>Horizontal:</div><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Horizontal Width="20rem" Class="pane">
                    <div class="chip-row">
                        @for (var i = 1; i <= 12; i++)
                        {
                            <div class="chip">Item @i</div>
                        }
                    </div>
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Overscroll" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
                <div>
                    What a scroll does once the pane has reached its edge. <b>Auto</b> carries it on into whatever scrolls
                    behind the pane; <b>Contain</b> stops it there; <b>None</b> also drops the rubber band and
                    pull-to-refresh - the setting for a pane in a dialog or a sheet. <b>OverscrollX</b> and
                    <b>OverscrollY</b> set one axis. Scroll the inner pane to its end and keep going.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitChoiceGroup @bind-Value="overscroll"
                                Horizontal
                                Label="Overscroll"
                                TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitOverscroll>" TValue="BitOverscroll">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Auto" Value="BitOverscroll.Auto" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Contain" Value="BitOverscroll.Contain" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="None" Value="BitOverscroll.None" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="12rem" Width="20rem" Class="pane">
                    <div class="item">The outer pane</div>
                    <BitScrollablePane Height="8rem" Overscroll="overscroll" Class="pane">
                        @for (var i = 1; i <= 12; i++)
                        {
                            <div class="item">Inner @i</div>
                        }
                    </BitScrollablePane>
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 6; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Outer @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Scrollbar" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
                <div>
                    The platform's scrollbar, tuned with the standard CSS properties. <b>ScrollbarWidth</b> is <b>Auto</b>,
                    <b>Thin</b> or <b>None</b> (hidden, still scrollable). <b>ScrollbarColor</b> takes the thumb and then
                    the track color - both, or the browser ignores it. <b>Gutter</b> reserves the bar's room so the content
                    does not shift when it appears: <b>Stable</b>, or <b>BothEdges</b> to keep the content centered.
                    Overlay scrollbars (macOS, mobile) take no room, so there is nothing for it to reserve.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitChoiceGroup @bind-Value="scrollbarWidth"
                                Horizontal
                                Label="ScrollbarWidth"
                                TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitScrollbarWidth>" TValue="BitScrollbarWidth">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Auto" Value="BitScrollbarWidth.Auto" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Thin" Value="BitScrollbarWidth.Thin" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="None" Value="BitScrollbarWidth.None" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <BitChoiceGroup @bind-Value="gutter"
                                Horizontal
                                Label="Gutter"
                                TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitScrollbarGutter>" TValue="BitScrollbarGutter">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Auto" Value="BitScrollbarGutter.Auto" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Stable" Value="BitScrollbarGutter.Stable" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="BothEdges" Value="BitScrollbarGutter.BothEdges" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap Gap="1rem">
                    <BitToggle @bind-Value="scrollbarColored" Label="ScrollbarColor" />
                    <BitToggle @bind-Value="scrollbarOverflowing" Label="Overflowing" />
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="12rem" Width="20rem" Class="pane"
                                   ScrollbarWidth="scrollbarWidth"
                                   Gutter="gutter"
                                   ScrollbarColor="@(scrollbarColored ? "var(--bit-clr-pri) var(--bit-clr-bg-ter)" : null)">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= (scrollbarOverflowing ? 10 : 3); i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Item @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Modern" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
                <div>
                    <b>Modern</b> draws a thin rounded thumb over a transparent track in the theme's colors, the same on
                    every platform (Firefox gets its thin standard bar in the same colors). <b>AutoHideScrollbar</b> paints
                    it only while the pane is pointed at, holds the focus, or has just been scrolled - so it is never
                    hidden from a keyboard or touch reader - and <b>AutoHideDelay</b> (ms, 800) is how long it lingers
                    after that. In forced colors mode the pane keeps the system scrollbar.
                    Chromium lets <b>ScrollbarWidth</b> and <b>ScrollbarColor</b> win over Modern, so use one or the other.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="autoHideScrollbar" Label="AutoHideScrollbar" />
                <BitNumberField Label="AutoHideDelay (ms)" Min="0" Step="200" @bind-Value="autoHideDelay" Style="max-width: 20rem" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="10rem" Class="pane" Modern AutoHideScrollbar="autoHideScrollbar" AutoHideDelay="(int)autoHideDelay">
                    <p>
                        Every story starts with a blank canvas, a quiet space waiting to be filled with ideas, emotions, and dreams.
                        These placeholder words symbolize the beginning - a moment of possibility where creativity has yet to take shape.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In the beginning, there is silence, a blank canvas yearning to be filled, a quiet space where creativity waits
                        to awaken. Think of this text as a bridge, connecting the empty spaces of now with the narratives of tomorrow.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In this space, potential reigns supreme. It is a moment suspended in time, where imagination dances freely and
                        each word has the power to transform into something extraordinary.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For now, these lines are here to remind you of the beauty of beginnings. They are the quiet before the symphony,
                        the foundation upon which your creativity will build.
                    </p>
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br /><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Horizontal Width="20rem" Class="pane" Modern AutoHideScrollbar="autoHideScrollbar" AutoHideDelay="(int)autoHideDelay">
                    <div class="chip-row">
                        @for (var i = 1; i <= 12; i++)
                        {
                            <div class="chip">Item @i</div>
                        }
                    </div>
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Fade" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
                <div>
                    <b>Fade</b> fades each edge that still has content beyond it, so the pane shows which way there is
                    more; <b>FadeSize</b> sets how far it reaches (2rem). It is a mask, so it works over any background -
                    and also fades a classic scrollbar and sticky content in the band. Pair it with a scrollbar that is
                    out of sight: <b>ScrollbarWidth="None"</b>, or <b>Modern</b> with <b>AutoHideScrollbar</b>. It is not
                    drawn under <i>prefers-contrast: more</i> or forced colors, where faded text would lose contrast.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="fade" Label="Fade" />
                <BitSlider Label="FadeSize (rem)" Min="0.5" Max="5" Step="0.5" @bind-Value="fadeSize" Style="max-width: 20rem" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="10rem" Class="pane" Modern AutoHideScrollbar
                                   Fade="fade" FadeSize="@($"{fadeSize}rem")">
                    <p>
                        Every story starts with a blank canvas, a quiet space waiting to be filled with ideas, emotions, and dreams.
                        These placeholder words symbolize the beginning - a moment of possibility where creativity has yet to take shape.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In the beginning, there is silence, a blank canvas yearning to be filled, a quiet space where creativity waits
                        to awaken. Think of this text as a bridge, connecting the empty spaces of now with the narratives of tomorrow.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In this space, potential reigns supreme. It is a moment suspended in time, where imagination dances freely and
                        each word has the power to transform into something extraordinary.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For now, these lines are here to remind you of the beauty of beginnings. They are the quiet before the symphony,
                        the foundation upon which your creativity will build.
                    </p>
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br /><br />
                <div>Both axes:</div><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="10rem" Width="20rem" Class="pane" ScrollbarWidth="BitScrollbarWidth.None" Fade="fade">
                    <div class="wide-grid">
                        @for (var row = 1; row <= 8; row++)
                        {
                            <div class="chip-row">
                                @for (var col = 1; col <= 8; col++)
                                {
                                    <div class="chip">R@(row)C@(col)</div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Keyboard & accessibility" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
                <div>
                    A pane with nothing focusable inside cannot be scrolled with the keyboard. <b>Focusable</b> makes the
                    pane itself a tab stop (WCAG 2.1.1); the arrows, Page Up/Down, Home and End then scroll it. Name it with
                    <b>AriaLabel</b>, which makes it a <i>region</i> unless <b>Role</b> says otherwise. Leave it off when
                    the content already has links or fields. A <b>TabIndex</b> also makes it focusable, and a disabled
                    pane leaves the tab order. The focus ring stays visible on a pane that fades.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="focusable" Label="Focusable" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="10rem" Class="pane" Fade
                                   Focusable="focusable"
                                   AriaLabel="Release notes">
                    <p>
                        Every story starts with a blank canvas, a quiet space waiting to be filled with ideas, emotions, and dreams.
                        These placeholder words symbolize the beginning - a moment of possibility where creativity has yet to take shape.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In the beginning, there is silence, a blank canvas yearning to be filled, a quiet space where creativity waits
                        to awaken. Think of this text as a bridge, connecting the empty spaces of now with the narratives of tomorrow.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        In this space, potential reigns supreme. It is a moment suspended in time, where imagination dances freely and
                        each word has the power to transform into something extraordinary.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For now, these lines are here to remind you of the beauty of beginnings. They are the quiet before the symphony,
                        the foundation upon which your creativity will build.
                    </p>
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Scroll events" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
                <div>
                    <b>OnScroll</b> reports a <b>BitScrollOffset</b>: both offsets, the content and visible sizes, derived
                    answers (<b>PercentX</b> / <b>PercentY</b>, <b>AtTop</b>, <b>AtBottom</b>, ...) and the move since the
                    last report (<b>DeltaTop</b>, <b>ScrollingDown</b>, ...). It also fires when a resize changes what the
                    position means. Reports are coalesced per frame; <b>ScrollThrottle</b> (ms) spaces them further, and
                    the last one always arrives. <b>OnScrollStart</b> and <b>OnScrollEnd</b> bracket each scroll with one
                    call each - the place for work too costly to run while scrolling.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitNumberField Label="ScrollThrottle (ms)" Min="0" Step="50" @bind-Value="scrollThrottle" Style="max-width: 20rem" />
                <br />
                <BitProgress Value="@((scrollOffset?.PercentY ?? 0) * 100)" AriaLabel="Scroll progress" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="12rem" Class="pane"
                                   ScrollThrottle="(int)scrollThrottle"
                                   OnScroll="HandleScroll"
                                   OnScrollStart="HandleScrollStart"
                                   OnScrollEnd="HandleScrollEnd">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 20; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Row @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br />
                <div class="readout">
                    Top: @((scrollOffset?.Top ?? 0).ToString("0")) of @((scrollOffset?.MaxTop ?? 0).ToString("0"))
                    &nbsp;|&nbsp; PercentY: @(((scrollOffset?.PercentY ?? 0) * 100).ToString("0"))%
                    &nbsp;|&nbsp; AtTop: @(scrollOffset?.AtTop.ToString() ?? "-")
                    &nbsp;|&nbsp; AtBottom: @(scrollOffset?.AtBottom.ToString() ?? "-")
                </div>
                <div class="readout">State: @scrollState &nbsp;|&nbsp; Going: @scrollDirection</div>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Reaching the edges" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
                <div>
                    <b>OnReachedTop</b>, <b>OnReachedBottom</b>, <b>OnReachedLeft</b> and <b>OnReachedRight</b> fire once
                    per arrival at an edge (the horizontal two are the screen's edges, in RTL too). <b>ReachOffset</b> is
                    how near counts as reached - give an endless list a screenful so the next page arrives in time. The
                    edges a pane starts at, and both edges of an axis with nothing to scroll, are never reported.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitNumberField Label="ReachOffset (px)" Min="0" Step="20" @bind-Value="reachOffset" Style="max-width: 20rem" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="14rem" Class="pane"
                                   ReachOffset="(int)reachOffset"
                                   OnReachedTop="HandleReachedTop"
                                   OnReachedBottom="LoadMoreRows">
                    @foreach (var row in endlessRows)
                    {
                        <div class="item">@row</div>
                    }
                    @if (loadingMore)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Loading...</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br />
                <div class="readout">Last edge reached: <b>@reachedEdge</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; rows: <b>@endlessRows.Count</b></div>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Scrolling from code" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
                <div>
                    <b>ScrollToStart</b>, <b>ScrollToEnd</b>, <b>ScrollTo</b> (a null axis stays put), <b>ScrollBy</b>,
                    <b>ScrollToElement</b> (an id or an <b>ElementReference</b>, with an <b>alignment</b>: Start, Center,
                    End or Nearest) and
                    <b>GetScrollOffset</b>. Each takes an optional <b>smooth</b>, defaulting to the <b>Smooth</b> parameter.
                    <b>ScrollToElement</b> moves only the pane, never the page, and keeps the room <b>ScrollPadding</b>
                    asks for - here 2.5rem under a sticky header. <b>FocusAsync</b> focuses the pane; <b>Refresh</b>
                    re-measures it after a change no observer sees (a web font, an image).
                </div>
                <br />
                <div>
                    <b>InitialScrollTop</b> / <b>InitialScrollLeft</b> open the pane at a saved position, once and without
                    animation.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="smooth" Label="Smooth" />
                <br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap Gap="0.5rem">
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => scrollablePane?.ScrollToStart()">To start</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => scrollablePane?.ScrollToEnd()">To end</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => scrollablePane?.ScrollTo(null, 200)">To 200px</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => scrollablePane?.ScrollBy(0, 100)">Down 100px</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick='() => scrollablePane?.ScrollToElement("scp-row-15")'>To row 15</BitButton>
                    <BitButton OnClick='() => scrollablePane?.ScrollToElement("scp-row-15", alignment: BitScrollAlignment.Center)'>To row 15, centered</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="ReadScrollOffset">Read position</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => scrollablePane?.FocusAsync()">Focus the pane</BitButton>
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane @ref="scrollablePane" Height="12rem" Class="pane" Smooth="smooth"
                                   ScrollPadding="2.5rem" Focusable AriaLabel="Rows">
                    <div class="sticky-head">A sticky header, with 2.5rem of ScrollPadding under it</div>
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 25; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item" id="@($"scp-row-{i}")">Row @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br />
                <div class="readout">@readPosition</div>
                <br /><br />
                <div>InitialScrollTop="250":</div><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="8rem" Class="pane" InitialScrollTop="250">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 25; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Row @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="AutoScroll" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
                <div>
                    <b>AutoScroll</b> keeps the pane pinned to the end as content grows - a chat, a log, a console. Scroll
                    up and arriving lines stop pulling you down; scroll back to the end and the pinning resumes.
                    <b>AutoScrollThreshold</b> (px) is how near the end still counts as at it. <b>Smooth</b> animates the moves.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitNumberField Label="AutoScrollThreshold (px)" Min="0" Step="10" @bind-Value="autoScrollThreshold" Style="max-width: 20rem" />
                <br />
                <BitButton OnClick="AddAutoScrollContent" IsEnabled="@(autoScrollRunning is false)">Add lines periodically</BitButton>
                <br /><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="14rem" Class="pane" AutoScroll AutoScrollThreshold="(int)autoScrollThreshold">
                    <div class="item">The log starts here.</div>
                    @foreach (var line in autoScrollLines)
                    {
                        <div class="item">@line</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Preserving the reader's place" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
                <div>
                    Older messages loaded above the reader push what they were reading down the screen.
                    <b>PreserveScroll</b> moves the pane down by exactly what arrived, so nothing they see moves. Only
                    content above the visible area counts, and a pane at the very top is left alone - which makes it safe to
                    pair with <b>OnReachedTop</b>. Browsers with CSS scroll anchoring already do this; the parameter brings
                    Safari up to them. The pane below opts out of anchoring (<i>overflow-anchor: none</i>) so the toggle
                    shows a difference everywhere.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="preserveScroll" Label="PreserveScroll" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="14rem" Class="pane no-anchor" Fade
                                   PreserveScroll="preserveScroll"
                                   ReachOffset="60"
                                   OnReachedTop="LoadOlderMessages">
                    @if (loadingOlder)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Loading older messages...</div>
                    }
                    @* Keyed, so the older messages are new elements at the top rather than new text in the old ones. *@
                    @foreach (var message in conversation)
                    {
                        <div @key="message" class="item">@message</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br />
                <div class="readout">Oldest message loaded: <b>@oldestMessage</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; messages: <b>@conversation.Count</b></div>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Snapping" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
                <div>
                    <b>Snap</b> makes the pane come to rest on its items - <b>Mandatory</b> always, <b>Proximity</b> when
                    near one. <b>SnapAlign</b> (Start, Center, End) sets the snap position of the pane's direct children;
                    items inside a wrapper of your own need their own <i>scroll-snap-align</i>. <b>SnapStop</b> stops at
                    the next item however hard the flick - a carousel. Prefer Proximity for a pane that is also scrolled
                    from code.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitChoiceGroup @bind-Value="snap"
                                Horizontal
                                Label="Snap"
                                TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitScrollSnap>" TValue="BitScrollSnap">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="None" Value="BitScrollSnap.None" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Proximity" Value="BitScrollSnap.Proximity" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Mandatory" Value="BitScrollSnap.Mandatory" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <BitChoiceGroup @bind-Value="snapAlign"
                                Horizontal
                                Label="SnapAlign"
                                TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitScrollSnapAlign>" TValue="BitScrollSnapAlign">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Start" Value="BitScrollSnapAlign.Start" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Center" Value="BitScrollSnapAlign.Center" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="End" Value="BitScrollSnapAlign.End" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="snapStop" Label="SnapStop" />
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Horizontal Width="22rem" Class="pane" Modern
                                   Snap="snap" SnapAlign="snapAlign" SnapStop="snapStop">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="card">Card @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Dragging & the wheel" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example15CsharpCode" Id="example15">
                <div>
                    For a mouse, which has no easy way to scroll sideways. <b>DragScroll</b> scrolls the pane by dragging
                    its content; a click is still a click, and drags starting on fields, links, buttons or anything marked
                    <b>data-bit-scp-nodrag</b> are left alone. <b>DragMomentum</b> lets a released drag glide to a stop.
                    <b>HorizontalWheel</b> turns the vertical wheel into a sideways scroll for a pane that only scrolls
                    sideways, and hands it back to the page at the end.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitStack Horizontal Wrap Gap="1rem">
                    <BitToggle @bind-Value="dragScroll" Label="DragScroll" />
                    <BitToggle @bind-Value="dragMomentum" Label="DragMomentum" />
                    <BitToggle @bind-Value="horizontalWheel" Label="HorizontalWheel" />
                </BitStack>
                <br />
                <BitScrollablePane Horizontal Width="22rem" Class="pane" Modern
                                   DragScroll="dragScroll" DragMomentum="dragMomentum"
                                   HorizontalWheel="horizontalWheel">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="card">Card @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="CSS variables" RazorCode="@example16RazorCode" Id="example16">
                <div>
                    The public <b>--bit-ScrollablePane-*</b> variables inherit: set them on <i>:root</i> for the whole app,
                    on a container for a region, or in <b>Style</b> for one pane. They restyle the <b>Modern</b> scrollbar
                    (size, thumb colors and corner, track), the <b>Fade</b> depth and the focus ring. <b>FadeSize</b> wins
                    over an inherited fade size.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <div class="themed-panes">
                    <BitScrollablePane Height="8rem" Class="pane" Modern Focusable AriaLabel="Themed pane">
                        @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                        {
                            <div class="item">Styled by the container @i</div>
                        }
                    </BitScrollablePane>
                    <br />
                    <BitScrollablePane Height="8rem" Class="pane" Modern Fade
                                       Style="--bit-ScrollablePane-scrollbar-thumb-color: var(--bit-clr-sec); --bit-ScrollablePane-fade-size: 3rem;">
                        @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                        {
                            <div class="item">Styled by its own Style @i</div>
                        }
                    </BitScrollablePane>
                </div>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example17RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example17CsharpCode" Id="example17">
                <div>
                    <b>BitParams</b> hands a <b>BitScrollablePaneParams</b> to every pane under it, so a set of panes shares
                    one setup. The values are defaults: a pane's own parameters win.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitParams Parameters="@scrollablePaneParams">
                    <BitStack Horizontal Wrap Gap="1rem">
                        <BitScrollablePane Width="14rem" Class="pane">
                            @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                            {
                                <div class="item">Cascaded @i</div>
                            }
                        </BitScrollablePane>
                        <BitScrollablePane Width="14rem" Class="pane" Fade="false" Height="6rem">
                            @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                            {
                                <div class="item">Own Fade, Height @i</div>
                            }
                        </BitScrollablePane>
                    </BitStack>
                </BitParams>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" Id="example18">
                <div>
                    The pane is a single element, so <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the box that scrolls.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <div>Component's Style:</div><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="8rem"
                                   Style="border: 2px solid var(--bit-clr-pri); border-radius: 0.5rem; padding: 0.5rem;">
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Item @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br /><br />
                <div>Component's Class:</div><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Height="8rem" Class="custom-pane" Modern>
                    @for (var i = 1; i <= 10; i++)
                    {
                        <div class="item">Item @i</div>
                    }
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>

            <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" Id="example19">
                <div>
                    With <b>Dir="BitDir.Rtl"</b>, names about the content follow the content - <b>ScrollToStart</b>,
                    <b>ScrollToEnd</b> and <b>AutoScroll</b> treat the visual left as the end - while names about the screen
                    follow the screen: <b>OnReachedLeft</b>, <b>ScrollTo</b>, <b>ScrollBy</b> and the offsets
                    <b>BitScrollOffset</b> reports all count from the visual left, so a read position can be handed back as is.
                </div>
                <br /><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Dir="BitDir.Rtl" lang="fa" Height="8rem" Class="pane" Modern>
                    <p>
                        داستان‌ها روزگاری پیوند میان مردم را می‌بافتند، سمفونی‌ای از صداها که رویاهای مشترک را می‌ساخت.
                        هر واژه معنایی داشت و هر مکث فهمی به همراه می‌آورد.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        در آغاز، سکوت است؛ بومی سفید که در انتظار پر شدن است، فضایی آرام که در آن خلاقیت منتظر بیدار شدن است.
                        این واژه‌ها موقتی‌اند و جای ایده‌هایی را گرفته‌اند که هنوز نیامده‌اند.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        در این فضا، امکان حکمرانی می‌کند. لحظه‌ای معلق در زمان، جایی که تخیل آزادانه می‌رقصد و هر واژه
                        توان آن را دارد که به چیزی خارق‌العاده بدل شود.
                    </p>
                </BitScrollablePane>
                <br /><br />
                <BitScrollablePane Dir="BitDir.Rtl" lang="fa" Horizontal Width="20rem" Class="pane" Fade>
                    داستان‌ها روزگاری پیوند میان مردم را می‌بافتند، سمفونی‌ای از صداها که رویاهای مشترک را می‌ساخت.
                </BitScrollablePane>
            </DemoExample>
        </Examples>
    </DemoPage>
</div>
